    "https://secure.entertimeonline.com/ta/ColbySawyer.careers?ShowJob=537215559": {
      "title": "ColbySawyer:Career Search",
      "description": "ColbySawyer:Career Search Skip to Main Content Log in Spin Instructor Show All Jobs Apply Show Map Location 541 Main Street,\n    \n                      New London, NH, 03257,\n                      United States Employee Type Non-Exempt PT < 20 hours Contact information Name Human Resources Phone 603.526.3704 Email cscjobs@colby-sawyer.edu Description We are seeking an energetic, engaging, and reliable Spin Instructor to lead dynamic indoor cycling classes for a diverse campus community. This position will work with both college students and community members, creating an inclusive, motivating, and welcoming environment for participants of varying fitness levels and experience. The ideal candidate is an enthusiastic fitness professional who can connect with a wide range of participants and bring energy and positivity to every class. Availability to teach or regularly cover our 6:00 a.m. class on Mondays, Wednesdays and Fridays is highly desirable. Responsibilities Lead safe, energetic, and engaging indoor cycling/Spin classes for students, faculty/staff, and community members. Provide clear instruction on cycling technique, resistance, cadence, positioning, and overall class flow. Create motivating playlists and class formats appropriate for a diverse range of fitness levels. Offer modifications and options to accommodate participants with different abilities and experience levels. Foster an inclusive and welcoming atmosphere that encourages participants to return and stay active. Arrive on time and prepared for each scheduled class, including early-morning sessions. Follow campus recreation policies, safety procedures, and emergency protocols. Maintain a professional and positive presence while representing the campus recreation program. Communicate effectively with recreation staff regarding attendance, equipment, scheduling, and participant feedback. Requirements Qualifications Required: Previous experience teaching indoor cycling/Spin classes or a comparable group fitness format. Current nationally recognized group fitness and/or indoor cycling certification, or the ability to obtain certification as required. Current CPR/AED certification or the ability to obtain it within the required timeframe. Strong communication, interpersonal, and motivational skills. Ability to create an inclusive and positive environment for participants of varying ages and fitness levels. Dependable, punctual, and professional. Preferred: Experience working with a diverse population of students and community members. Availability for 6:00 a.m. classes and/or early-morning class coverage . Additional group fitness certifications or relevant fitness education Summary Colby-Sawyer College is a comprehensive liberal arts-based institution located in the scenic Lake Sunapee area of New Hampshire. We have friendly people, beautiful nature, and small-town amenities and shops. We are located about 35 minutes south of Dartmouth College and just 1 hour 30 minutes from Boston. For more information see Lake Sunapee Region Chamber of Commerce, NH . We strive to always be a people-first organization. We are passionate about building and sustaining an inclusive and equitable working environment for all employees. If you are looking for a place where you will feel welcome and that you belong, Colby-Sawyer College is that place. Colby-Sawyer is especially interested in candidates who can contribute to the diversity and excellence of the institution. Applicants are encouraged to share what they can offer and how they can help us to achieve and maintain these values in their cover letter. Interested candidates may also visit Human Resources in Colgate Hall Room 230, Monday through Friday from 8am - 5pm, to alternatively complete a paper application. Review of candidates will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Colby-Sawyer College is committed to maintaining a diverse workforce and an inclusive work environment which celebrates multiple perspectives. Under institutional policy, as well as under state and federal law (including Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972 and the Age Discrimination Act), Colby-Sawyer College does not discriminate in its hiring or employment practices or its admission practices on the basis of race or ethnicity; color; national origin; religion; age; mental or physical disability; parental, family or marital status; veteran status; or sex (including pregnancy or related conditions, sexual orientation, genetic information, gender identity or gender expression). Further, Colby-Sawyer College prohibits sex discrimination in any education program or activity that it operates. The college recognizes that harassment related to an individual's sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, or gender expression can occur in conjunction with misconduct related to an individual's race, color, ethnicity, national origin, religion, age, or disability. Targeting individuals on the basis of these characteristics is also a violation of the college policy. Under these circumstances, the college will coordinate the investigation and resolution efforts outlined in the college’s Sex-based Discrimination and Sex-based Harassment Policy and Grievance Procedures as well as the Code of Community Responsibility (for students) and Employee Handbook (for employees), to address harassment related to the targeted individual's sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, or gender expression together with the conduct related to the targeted individual’s race, color, ethnicity, national origin, religion, age or disability."
    },
    "https://secure.entertimeonline.com/ta/ColbySawyer.careers?ShowJob=537213376": {
      "title": "ColbySawyer:Career Search",
      "description": "ColbySawyer:Career Search Skip to Main Content Log in Windy Hill School - Extended Day Teacher Show All Jobs Apply Show Map Location 541 Main Street,\n    \n                      New London, NH, 03257,\n                      United States Employee Type Non-Exempt PT < 20 hours Contact information Name Human Resources Phone 603.526.3704 Email CSCjobs@colby-sawyer.edu Description Extended Day Team Teacher Windy Hill School at Colby-Sawyer college is a living learning community where children ages 13 months–5 years are seen as capable, curious, and creative — co-constructors of knowledge and meaning. Rooted in the Reggio Emilia approach and nourished by the natural world, our program immerses children in open-ended, nature-rich experiences that honor the hundred languages of children. As a college laboratory school, we are simultaneously a place of practice, mentorship, and ongoing inquiry. Team Teachers work alongside college students and faculty in a reflective, collaborative environment that benefits both children and the next generation of early childhood professionals. Role Overview The Extended Day Team Teacher is a hands-on, collaborative member of the classroom teaching team. You will help co-construct the learning environment, document children’s thinking and play, facilitate small-group outdoor exploration, and nurture a warm, responsive community grounded in respectful relationships. This role is ideal for someone who finds wonder in the small things — a caterpillar on a leaf, a puddle after rain, a child’s theory about shadows — and who understands that the teacher’s role is to listen deeply, provoke thinking, and follow the child’s lead. Environment & Curriculum Co-design indoor and outdoor learning environments that invite inquiry, sensory exploration, and connection with nature Contribute to emergent, project-based curriculum grounded in children’s interests and the Reggio philosophy Child Observation & Documentation Observe and document children’s play, language, and theories through photos, anecdotal notes, and video Contribute to learning stories, pedagogical panels, and journey books that make children’s thinking visible Participate in bi-weekly team documentation review and reflective dialogue Relationship & Community Build warm, trusting relationships with children, families, and colleagues Support daily routines — arrival, mealtimes, rest, outdoor time — with intentionality and care Communicate respectfully with families through informal daily exchanges School Year Part Time Position as Extended Day Toddler Teacher:  Hours 12:00-5:30, M-F. Full time, 40 hours per week M-F as a team teacher in our 9-week summer program. Other Responsibilities include participating in staff meetings, professional development, and reflection as an engaged member of the Windy Hill teaching team. Requirements Must meet state licensing requirements for early childhood staff (background check, health clearances, first aid CPR certification) Demonstrated commitment to responsive and equitable early childhood practice Excellent written and verbal communication skills — warm, professional, and clear Comfort in learning new technology systems Associate's degree or higher in Early Childhood Education or a related field (Bachelor's preferred) Preferred: Minimum 2 years of experience in an early childhood setting, ideally in a lab school, Reggio-inspired, or nature-based program Familiarity with Pyramid Model Practices Background in nature-based education, forest school, or outdoor learning environments Familiarity with Reggio Emilia philosophy, documentation practices, and emergent curriculum Summary Colby-Sawyer College is a comprehensive liberal arts-based institution located in the scenic Lake Sunapee area of New Hampshire. We have friendly people, beautiful nature, and small-town amenities and shops. We are located about 35 minutes south of Dartmouth College and just 1 hour 30 minutes from Boston. For more information see Lake Sunapee Region Chamber of Commerce, NH . We strive to always be a people-first organization. We are passionate about building and sustaining an inclusive and equitable working environment for all employees. If you are looking for a place where you will feel welcome and that you belong, Colby-Sawyer College is that place. Colby-Sawyer is especially interested in candidates who can contribute to the diversity and excellence of the institution. Applicants are encouraged to share what they can offer and how they can help us to achieve and maintain these values in their cover letter. Interested candidates may also visit Human Resources in Colgate Hall Room 230, Monday through Friday from 8am - 5pm, to alternatively complete a paper application. Review of candidates will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Colby-Sawyer College is proud to be an equal opportunity employer. We are committed to maintaining a diverse workforce and an inclusive work environment which celebrates multiple perspectives. Colby-Sawyer College will not tolerate discrimination in employment or employment-related decisions on the basis of race, ethnicity, color, national origin, gender, religion, age, mental or physical disability, family or marital status, sexual orientation, veteran status, genetic information, or gender identity."
    },
    "https://secure.entertimeonline.com/ta/ColbySawyer.careers?ShowJob=537200448": {
      "title": "ColbySawyer:Career Search",
      "description": "ColbySawyer:Career Search Skip to Main Content Log in Director of Undergraduate Admissions Show All Jobs Apply Show Map Location 541 Main Street,\n    \n                      New London, NH, 03257,\n                      United States Employee Type Exempt FT Travel 25% Contact information Name Human Resources Phone 603.526.3704 Email CSCjobs@colby-sawyer.edu Description Summary The Director of Undergraduate Admissions works closely with the Vice President for Enrollment Management to achieve the College's undergraduate enrollment goals. The Director leads the Admissions team and is responsible for implementing, executing, and managing recruitment and admissions strategies and initiatives. This position oversees day-to-day admissions operations, monitors progress toward enrollment and revenue goals and ensures effective follow-through across the recruitment and admission cycle. Essential Job Functions · Provide leadership in the development, implementation, and execution of a dynamic undergraduate search, recruitment, and admission strategy aligned with the College's strategic enrollment and revenue goals. · Lead recruitment efforts to increase the size, quality, diversity, and geographic reach of the undergraduate applicant pool. · Identify and evaluate new markets, populations, programs, and recruitment opportunities at the local, regional, national, and international levels. · Establish recruitment priorities and performance expectations for the Admissions team and monitor progress throughout the recruitment cycle. · Analyze enrollment data, market trends, recruitment activity, and outcomes to inform decisions and recommend adjustments to recruitment strategies. · Prepare and present comprehensive weekly and monthly enrollment reports, forecasts, and analyses, including strategy, progress, policy, and procedure updates, to the Vice President for Enrollment Management and other campus leaders. · Oversee undergraduate recruitment and admission activities, including prospect engagement, application review, admission decisions, scholarship consideration, yield, and enrollment conversion. · Oversee recruitment territories, travel planning, counselor activity, school and community outreach, and other student engagement strategies. · Work closely with the Vice President for Enrollment Management and Director of Financial Aid to implement enrollment, financial aid, and net tuition revenue strategies. · Collaborate with academic leaders and campus partners to support recruitment and enrollment initiatives for academic programs and institutional priorities. · Develop, evaluate, and implement Admissions policies, procedures, and practices that support an efficient and student-centered admission process. · Recruit, hire, train, supervise, and evaluate Admissions staff; establish performance expectations and provide ongoing coaching and professional development. · Foster a collaborative, student-centered, and results-oriented culture within the Admissions Office and ensure effective coordination of team responsibilities. · Collaborate with outside vendors and Marketing and Communications to develop and evaluate recruitment communications, digital strategies, advertising, and other enrollment materials. · Oversee and evaluate campus visit programs, recruitment events, prospective student engagement, and other prospective student and family experiences. · Serve as the primary lead for planning and implementing admissions events, collaborating with the Vice President for Enrollment Management to develop event strategy and content. · Plan and implement all admissions events, from initial concept through execution, to support recruitment and enrollment goals. · Collaborate with Financial Aid, Academic Affairs, Student Success and Retention, Athletics, Marketing and Communications, and other campus partners to advance enrollment goals; represent the College with external constituents and professional organizations. · Manage Admissions resources and assess the effectiveness of recruitment activities, including travel, events, technology, and communications; participate in strategic enrollment projects and other duties as assigned. Requirements · Bachelor's degree required; master's degree preferred. · Five or more years of progressively responsible experience in undergraduate admissions, enrollment management, recruitment, or a related field, preferably within a private, liberal arts college. · Demonstrated success in student recruitment and enrollment, with experience developing and implementing strategies that support institutional enrollment goals. · Demonstrated leadership experience, including supervising, developing, and motivating professional staff. · Strong analytical and organizational skills, with experience using enrollment data, metrics, forecasting, and reporting to inform recruitment strategies and decisions. · Experience with CRM systems, enrollment management technology, and digital tools used to support student recruitment and engagement. · Knowledge of undergraduate admissions practices, recruitment trends, financial aid, and net tuition revenue strategies. · Strong communication, presentation, interpersonal, collaboration, and relationship-building skills, with the ability to work effectively with a wide range of internal and external constituencies. · Ability to manage multiple priorities, exercise sound judgment, adapt to changing enrollment conditions, and effectively move initiatives from planning through implementation and follow-through. Ability and willingness to travel and participate in evening and weekend recruitment activities and events as needed. Summary Colby-Sawyer College is a comprehensive liberal arts-based institution located in the scenic Lake Sunapee area of New Hampshire. We have friendly people, beautiful nature, and small-town amenities and shops. We are located about 35 minutes south of Dartmouth College and just 1 hour 30 minutes from Boston. For more information see Lake Sunapee Region Chamber of Commerce, NH . We strive to always be a people-first organization. We are passionate about building and sustaining an inclusive and equitable working environment for all employees. If you are looking for a place where you will feel welcome and that you belong, Colby-Sawyer College is that place. Colby-Sawyer is especially interested in candidates who can contribute to the diversity and excellence of the institution. Applicants are encouraged to share what they can offer and how they can help us to achieve and maintain these values in their cover letter. Interested candidates may also visit Human Resources in Colgate Hall Room 230, Monday through Friday from 8am - 5pm, to alternatively complete a paper application. Review of candidates will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Colby-Sawyer College is committed to maintaining a diverse workforce and an inclusive work environment which celebrates multiple perspectives. Under institutional policy, as well as under state and federal law (including Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972 and the Age Discrimination Act), Colby-Sawyer College does not discriminate in its hiring or employment practices or its admission practices on the basis of race or ethnicity; color; national origin; religion; age; mental or physical disability; parental, family or marital status; veteran status; or sex (including pregnancy or related conditions, sexual orientation, genetic information, gender identity or gender expression). Further, Colby-Sawyer College prohibits sex discrimination in any education program or activity that it operates. The college recognizes that harassment related to an individual's sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, or gender expression can occur in conjunction with misconduct related to an individual's race, color, ethnicity, national origin, religion, age, or disability. Targeting individuals on the basis of these characteristics is also a violation of the college policy. Under these circumstances, the college will coordinate the investigation and resolution efforts outlined in the college’s Sex-based Discrimination and Sex-based Harassment Policy and Grievance Procedures as well as the Code of Community Responsibility (for students) and Employee Handbook (for employees), to address harassment related to the targeted individual's sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, or gender expression together with the conduct related to the targeted individual’s race, color, ethnicity, national origin, religion, age or disability."
    },
    "https://secure.entertimeonline.com/ta/ColbySawyer.careers?ShowJob=520485313": {
      "title": "ColbySawyer:Career Search",
      "description": "ColbySawyer:Career Search Skip to Main Content Log in Director/Career Svcs/BOLD Show All Jobs Apply Show Map Location 541 Main Street,\n    \n                      New London, NH, 03257,\n                      United States Employee Type Exempt FT Description Summary To develop and implement a college-wide program of integrated services designed to assist and coach our diverse population of students to find their true academic and vocational interests, prepare students for entry into the workplace, and to discover further educational opportunities after graduation. Serve as coordinator for all services provided through the Harrington Center for Experiential Learning. The position plays a key liaison role between students in need of career preparation and development services and the support network available to them (faculty, staff, graduate schools, internship sites, health & counseling, library, residential education, etc.). The Harrington Center also has oversight of coordinating vocational and community engaged learning efforts across the campus. Position reports to Vice President for Student Development & Dean of Students. Essential Job Functions Establish and implement a campus-wide plan for a Career Preparation Program, integrating academic and co-curricular support services, so that career education is understood and utilized across the curriculum whenever possible. Serve as contact person for all students, staff, and faculty on issues concerning career preparation and development, including the identification of aptitudes, obstacles, and services available. Receive, process and/or assign, and track all requests for information, equipment, service, or technical assistance. Direct strategic planning process for department and ensure implementation and achievement of departmental goals and programmatic changes. Create and maintain intuitive website of all services for students, alumni, faculty, and employers. Maintain processes and function of online career tool Handshake including posting jobs, events and career development tools for students and alumni. Require knowledge of Career Development resources in use at CSC (assessment tools such as FocusV2 ) in addition to online-related resources such as ONET , and local and national Labor Market Information and Resources (such as https://www.nhes.nh.gov/elmi/ ). Lead faculty and other college personnel in a consolidated effort to integrate career services, vocational learning and community engaged learning into classroom and co-curricular experiences. Provide services, training and resources to faculty and staff to assist in their support of student professional development and academic outcomes. Provide data to all departments for program reviews including statistical data on graduate employment/education placements and rates and internship placements and outcomes. (Specifically collecting data and producing the annual six-month Post-Graduate Survey, receiving help with survey distribution from the alumni office.) Develop relationships and partnerships with local and national institutions and industries for cooperative internship ventures. Assure proper documentation (review for ethical and legal issues) before internship approvals, and monitor personal and professional progress throughout internship experience. Establish methods for evaluating qualitative and statistical success of internship program. Maintain current online library and web resources of jobs, internship possibilities, graduate schools, alumni network with partnership with alumni office, and other career resources. Oversee and assure establishment of individual career plans for each student seeking employability skills, coordinating with faculty, academic advisors, and internship sites as needed. Incorporate career development models where appropriate in career coaching. Special attention to providing one-on-one service to all BOLD Scholars every semester. Develop effective methods and materials for marketing and promotion of the Harrington Center and its services. Program management for all career development related programming on campus associated with the Harrington Center, including instruction of INT010 each spring. Perform general project management tasks associated with Career Services department. May include project tracking, ordering of goods and services, serving as liaison with other departments, clerical functions, and departmental reporting. Maintain basic understanding of technology available to students and keep current on best practices at CSC. Hire, train, supervise, and monitor performance of Career Development professional and support staff. Maintain confidentiality of all proprietary and confidential information/materials for department. ADDITIONAL* BOLD DIRECTOR RESPONSIBILITIES (As guided by BOLD Women’s Leadership Network and the Helen Gurley Brown Foundation ) Primary Responsibilities: Meet with BOLD Scholars at least once per month as a group to provide guidance with respect to   leadership, career development and networking Provide mentoring and support to individual BOLD Scholars as needed, minimum of once per semester Design, plan and attend annual BOLD retreat for CSC BOLD Scholars Oversee balance of budget and expenses for BOLD, provide periodic updates of budget lines as needed Gather evidence regarding program effectiveness Coordinate communication among relevant offices of CSC engages with BOLD support and programming Plan and hold meetings with BOLD Advisory Group and campus Lead, at least once per semester Produce annual report to the Foundation with Lead and President’s final approvals. Attend annual retreat with Leads and Directors from BOLD Schools Work with Scholars requesting internship grants Assist Scholars who wish to apply for a post-graduate fellowship, host presentations with BOLD Advisory Group and make awards Manages fellowships for following year for each post-grad BOLD fellow (and sometimes other HGB   Foundation fellows) If you are a part-time BOLD director on your campus, the oversight of a part-time BOLD assistant Maintain confidentiality of all proprietary and confidential information/materials for department. Requirements Qualifications Master’s Degree in Counseling, Student Services, Education, or other appropriate field. Minimum 5 years of progressively responsible experience in college or university career services or related field. Proven ability to work effectively with students, alumni, faculty, parents, employers and the community. Expertise in website development and maintenance as well as knowledge of career development programs, Microsoft Excel and PowerPoint, and MS Office programs preferred. Demonstrated leadership, supervisory, organizational and strategic planning experience. Superior written and verbal communication skills, including a proven ability to communicate with and assist a variety of customers and vendors successfully. Ability to develop and manage budgets, and direct multiple projects. Demonstrated program development and events coordination experience. Position will sometimes require a non-traditional schedule, involving some weekends, evening hours and occasional travel. Summary Colby-Sawyer College is a comprehensive liberal arts-based institution located in the scenic Lake Sunapee area of New Hampshire. We have friendly people, beautiful nature, and small-town amenities and shops. We are located about 35 minutes south of Dartmouth College and just 1 hour 30 minutes from Boston. For more information see Lake Sunapee Region Chamber of Commerce, NH . We strive to always be a people-first organization. We are passionate about building and sustaining an inclusive and equitable working environment for all employees. If you are looking for a place where you will feel welcome and that you belong, Colby-Sawyer College is that place. Colby-Sawyer is especially interested in candidates who can contribute to the diversity and excellence of the institution. Applicants are encouraged to share what they can offer and how they can help us to achieve and maintain these values in their cover letter. Interested candidates may also visit Human Resources in Colgate Hall Room 230, Monday through Friday from 8am - 5pm, to alternatively complete a paper application. Review of candidates will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Colby-Sawyer College is committed to maintaining a diverse workforce and an inclusive work environment which celebrates multiple perspectives. Under institutional policy, as well as under state and federal law (including Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972 and the Age Discrimination Act), Colby-Sawyer College does not discriminate in its hiring or employment practices or its admission practices on the basis of race or ethnicity; color; national origin; religion; age; mental or physical disability; parental, family or marital status; veteran status; or sex (including pregnancy or related conditions, sexual orientation, genetic information, gender identity or gender expression). Further, Colby-Sawyer College prohibits sex discrimination in any education program or activity that it operates. The college recognizes that harassment related to an individual's sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, or gender expression can occur in conjunction with misconduct related to an individual's race, color, ethnicity, national origin, religion, age, or disability. Targeting individuals on the basis of these characteristics is also a violation of the college policy. Under these circumstances, the college will coordinate the investigation and resolution efforts outlined in the college’s Sex-based Discrimination and Sex-based Harassment Policy and Grievance Procedures as well as the Code of Community Responsibility (for students) and Employee Handbook (for employees), to address harassment related to the targeted individual's sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, or gender expression together with the conduct related to the targeted individual’s race, color, ethnicity, national origin, religion, age or disability."
